{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792593287,"nonce":"AI0O2YlFoGsLKC7Zp8pi/iXYki8=","title":"TypeScript: move cold snapshot preparation off the execution path","message":"The shared lowered-project snapshot removes repeat native lowering, but its cold creation currently lowers and optimizes the entire open project synchronously. On the NUC's 1,000-function fixture (only f0 is called), one cold observation was 87.247 ms versus 19.487 ms for the previous AST-cache creation. Warm medians from nine samples were 8.281 ms HIR hit versus 14.551 ms AST hit. These are process wall times, not isolated HIR times. A separate instrumented cold run reported 20.75 ms in shared lower_program; inlining took 8.42 + 7.73 ms and scalar_replace_alloc took 9.41 + 0.19 ms. Tracing perturbs timings. LLVM was selected in both arms, with baseline threshold 2,000,000,000 and OSR disabled to isolate interpreter-first startup.\n\nLet normal cold execution start in the interpreter while a bounded worker prepares and atomically publishes the persistent cache. At process completion cancel queued/in-flight preparation without joining or waiting for cache completion, as requested. Cancellation must be cooperative at safe phase/function boundaries, and publication must remain atomic; an interrupted temporary file must never become a valid cache hit. Runtime destruction must be safe while the worker owns its inputs. Do not move borrowed runtime/plugin pointers into a detached worker, duplicate frontend lowering, or deep-clone complete AST/HIR just to populate the cache. Prefer sharing already-lowered immutable IR with ownership independent of the runtime. Existing process-global tier callbacks must not be used to couple cache preparation to another live runtime.\n\nAlso reduce the work: lower/optimize only required bodies where execution/export policy permits, rather than eagerly optimizing all unused functions. Preserve module initializer order, reachable side effects, host entry points, exported generic templates and later specialization. Execution-root policy must be part of cache identity if it changes the set of saved bodies; simply changing closed=false is not sufficient for a reusable open project. Keep check-only AST caching cheap. Short processes are allowed to exit before an HIR cache is ready; preserve a useful AST entry or provide an explicit prewarm path without blocking ordinary exit.\n\nValidate early-exit cancellation, runtime-drop safety, repeated and concurrent cache writers, source changes during preparation, corrupt/stale cache fallback, and interpreter -\u003e Cranelift -\u003e LLVM execution. Compare frozen binaries on an idle NUC without Cargo between samples; report cold wall time separately from warm hits and throughput. Avoid asserting that threading removes compiler work; measure CPU/allocations and contention too.\n\nParent: 0c26889a7cd2c4060eb94ef93fd808c2857089dbf714735ca52b5d071bdb1564.\nPrerequisite: 5a43b4f18366bd869a0234a8fe15dd01f65f6c951a039952e99aa2d91707573d.","files":null}]}